{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_8cMUICQIASLmeFL4eOu5EfGF","content":"1\t# An ontology from a stack of papers\n2\t\n3\tYou have a folder of research papers, and a question that crosses them:\n4\t**which methods descend from which?** A model that reads papers can\n5\tanswer quickly. The answers are fluent, and some of them are wrong. A\n6\tpaper may say a method was *evaluated*, not *built on*. It may quote\n7\tsomeone else's claim, or deny it. Two fields may give two different\n8\tmethods the same name.\n9\t\n10\tGND 0.4.0 lets you build one checked ontology over many papers. The agent\n11\tstill does the reading. GND keeps each claim tied to its passage, refuses\n12\tto join names nobody decided, and proves or refuses every answer. This\n13\tpage follows one stack of sixteen papers from eleven fields through three\n14\tacts.\n15\t\n16\t<div class=\"app-figure\" markdown=\"1\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"The three acts of the papers story\">\n17\t\n18\t![Sixteen papers from eleven fields flow through three acts. Act 1, who is who: keep who said each claim and the passage, review each statement, decide which names are the same method. Act 2, one vocabulary: read every paper the same way, merge spellings and check a catalogue, flag dates that run backwards. Act 3, depth and change: answer or say unknown, know what was not read, revise and give each claim a verdict.](../assets/applications/papers-journey.svg)\n19\t\n20\t</div>"}
